2 CONT ==[https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Behmer_nr_486#TAB_3 Noble family Behmer no. 486]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TAB 3'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Behm, ennobled Behmer (son of Jon Mickelsson Behm, tab 1), to Ytterbynäs and Behmersberg (Tuppen), both in Skuttunge parish, Uppsala county. Before the ennoblement he wrote himself Daniel Jonae Gewalius, later Gislenius (Gi
2 CONC slenus, Gissel), but from 1638 Behm. Born 1611 in Gävle. Student in Uppsala 1626-02-01. Eloquentiae professor there. 1640-11-16. Royal secretary 1645-01-14. County governor in Långhundra Uppsala county and Tuhundra Västmanland cou
2 CONC nty 1646-01-03. Assessor in Svea court of appeal 1649-04-17. Ennobled 1650-06-15, (introd. s. å. under no. 486). Secretary of State and commissioner in the reduction board 1655–1662. Court councilor 1664-12-14. Assistant in the Of
2 CONC fice of the Auditor General 1666-11-22. Died 1669-12-29 in Älvkarleby during a trip to Norrland, suddenly of a stroke and buried 1670-08-14 in Riddarholm Church in Stockholm, but later placed in the family grave in Skuttunge Churc
2 CONC h in Uppland. He was a tutor for the Chancellor of the Realm, Axel Oxenstierna's son Erik 1634–1638, which laid the foundation for his fortune [Bbl]. Married 1st 1643-07-26 to Brita Schytte, born 1623-11-10 in Nyköping, died 1668-
2 CONC 03-18, daughter of the councilor and merchant in Nyköping Henning Schytte and Engela Danckwardt, and sister of the chamber councilor Joakim Schytte, ennobled Skyttehielm , and the bishop Henning Skytte, ennobled Skyttenhielm . Mar
2 CONC ried 2nd 1669-04-24 to Magdalena Lindegren, in her 1st marriage (married 2nd to the equestrian master Carl Gustaf Lood in Småland , died 1704), born 1646-06-18, lived 1705, buried. in Hjortsberga church in Småland, daughter of the
2 CONC  assessor Nils Nilsson, ennobled Lindegren , and Anna Siöblad .
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Anna . Married 1667-06-04, in Stockholm to the registrar Anders Appelbom.'''
2 CONT * 1. '''Axel,''' born 1646. Assessor. Died 1693. See Tab. 4
2 CONT * 1. '''Ingegerd,''' baptized 1649-01-18 in Stockholm, died young.
2 CONT * 1. '''Jonas Henning,''' born 1650. Major. Died 1697. See Tab. 5
2 CONT * 1. '''Brita''', baptized 1659-08-12 in Stockholm. Married to Ensign Anders Magnus Eketrä .
2 CONT * 1. ''Daniel,'' born 1660 in Stockholm. Ensign in the Västerbotten Regiment 1681-07-01, died unmarried 1684-01-07 and buried in Skuttunge Church, where his coat of arms was erected. (Uff V p. 62.)
2 CONT * 1. '''Two daughters''', died young before their father.
2 CONT * 2. '''Nils,''' born twin 1670-06-25, posthumously (Uff V p. 62.) died 1671-02-22 and buried in Skuttunge church, where his coat of arms was erected. (Uff V p. 62.)
2 CONT * 2. '''Beata,''' born twin 1670-06-25, posthumous, still alive in 1735. Married in 1697 to Colonel Henrik Falkenhielm, no. 960, born 1673, died 1712.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Behmer Daniel Behmer, Swedish Lawyer and Civil Servant]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Behm , ennobled Behmer , born in October 1611 in Gävle , died on December 20, 1669 in Älvkarleby parish , was a Swedish jurist and civil servant .
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Behm's father Johan Mikaelsson Behm was mayor of the city of Gävle . His mother Anna Danielsdotter Kröger was from a large bourgeois family in Gävle and belonged to the Bureätten family .
2 CONT After Gävle school, Behm studied in Uppsala from 1629 , where he was given a position as an informant for Axel Oxenstierna's son Erik. This enabled him to undertake a study trip abroad in 1638–1640. Returning home in 1640, Queen C
2 CONC hristina appointed him professor of eloquentiae at Uppsala University , but five years later he left the university world for a mandate as royal secretary. The following year he became district governor in Långhundra and Tuhundra 
2 CONC . In 1649 he became assessor at the Svea Court of Appeal , in 1655 state secretary and commissioner in the Reduction College , from which he was dismissed in 1664. In 1666 Behmer received the title of court councilor and became a 
2 CONC member of the Justitierevisionsverket . Behmer wrote a panegyric poem about Johan Banér (1641). In 1650 he was ennobled and took the name Behmer and was granted a number of estates. He was introduced the same year at the House of 
2 CONC Knights with number 486. Behmer was buried by Nicolaus Johannis Rudbeckius in Riddarholmskyrkan , but the ashes were later moved to Skuttunge church .
2 CONT He was married twice. His first wife, the alderman's daughter Brita Schütte, was a commoner but her mother was a Danckwardt . With her he had eight children. His second wife, Magdalena Lindegren no. 338, bore him two children post
2 CONC humously, and as a widow remarried the cavalry master Carl Gustaf Lood. The Behmer family was established on the sword side with Daniel Behmer's grandson, the auditor Per Daniel Behmer, who died unmarried in 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
